WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.780
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:11.660
In 2017, so more than 300 episodes ago, we
talked about an algorithm that took a 3D model

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:17.030
of a complex object, and would give us an
easy to follow, step by step breakdown on

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:18.340
how to draw it.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.060
Automated drawing tutorials, if you will!

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.270
This was a handcrafted algorithm that used
graph theory to break these 3D objects into

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:31.660
smaller, easier to manage pieces, and since
then, learning algorithms have improved so

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.980
much that we started looking more and more
to the opposite direction.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.370
And that opposite direction would be giving
a crude drawing to the machine, and getting

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.670
a photorealistic image.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:48.530
Now that sounds like science fiction, until
we realize that scientists at NVIDIA already

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.930
had an amazing algorithm for this around 1.5
years ago.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:57.720
In that work, the input was a labeling which
we can draw ourselves, and the output is a

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.680
hopefully photorealistic landscape image that
adheres to these labels.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.190
I love how first, only the silhouette of the
rock is drawn, so we have this hollow thing

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:12.310
on the right that is not very realistic, and
then, it is now filled in with the bucket

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.070
tool, and, there you go.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:19.140
And next thing you know, you have an amazing-looking
landscape image.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.790
It was capable of much, much more, but what
it couldn’t do is synthesize human faces

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:25.990
this way.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.520
And believe it or not, this is what today’s
technique is able to do.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.520
Look!

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.890
In goes our crude sketch as a guide image,
and out comes a nearly photorealistic human

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.740
face that matches it.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.000
Interestingly, before we draw the hair itself,
it gives us something as a starting point,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.260
but if we choose to, we can also change the
hair shape and the outputs will follow our

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.810
drawing really well.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:56.530
But it goes much further than this as it boasts
a few additional appealing features.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.180
For instance, it not only refines the output
as we change our drawing, but since one crude

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:07.970
input can be mapped to many-many possible
people, these output images can also be further

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.120
art-directed with these sliders.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:15.420
According to the included user study, journeymen
users mainly appreciated the variety they

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.380
can achieve with this algorithm, if you look
here, you can get a taste of that, while professionals

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.610
were more excited about the controllability
aspect of this method.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.599
That was showcased with the footage with the
sliders.

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:33.610
Another really cool thing that it can do is
called face copy-paste, where we don’t even

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:38.349
need to draw anything, and just take a few
aspects of human faces that we would like

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:43.040
to combine, and there you go.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.989
Absolutely amazing.

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:47.740
This work is not without failure cases, however.

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:53.340
You have probably noticed, but the AI is not
explicitly instructed to match the eye colors,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.370
where some asymmetry may arise in the output.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.860
I am sure this will be improved just one more
paper down the line, and I am really curious

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.709
where digital artists will take these techniques
in the near future.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.820
The objective is always to get out of the
way, and help the artist spend more time bringing

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:14.840
their artistic vision to life, and spend less
time on the execution.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.090
This is exactly what these techniques can
help with.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:47.400
What a

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:58.959
time

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:08.209
to be alive!

00:04:08.209 --> 00:04:17.869
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

